%I A398668 #55 Oct 07 2026 01:44:54
%S A398668 1,3,7,26,74,322,725
%N A398668 Number of equivalence classes of dessins d'enfants with n edges under known Galois invariants.
%C A398668 This sequence counts equivalence classes of dessins d'enfants (bipartite graphs embedded on compact Riemann surfaces) with n edges, where two dessins are equivalent if they share the same passport (triple of cycle types of the monodromy permutations, in ordered form; that is, distinguishing (sigma_0, sigma_1) from (sigma_1, sigma_0)) and the same monodromy group order.
%C A398668 A dessin with n edges can be represented combinatorially as a transitive pair of permutations (sigma_0, sigma_1) in the symmetric group S_n. The passport is the triple of cycle types of sigma_0, sigma_1, and sigma_inf = (sigma_0*sigma_1)^{-1}. The monodromy group order is the size of the group generated by sigma_0 and sigma_1. The genus is determined by the passport via the Riemann-Hurwitz formula. The equivalence classes are computed by enumerating all transitive pairs and extracting the passport and monodromy group order.
%C A398668 For n <= 4, the Galois action is trivial (all dessins are defined over Q), so a(n) equals the number of dessins (A057005), which also equals the number of distinct passports.
%C A398668 For n >= 5, dessins with the same passport may lie in different Galois orbits due to having different monodromy group structures. This sequence counts classes under known Galois invariants, which are necessary but not always sufficient to distinguish Galois orbits.
%C A398668 This sequence is inspired by Grothendieck's "Esquisse d'un Programme" (1984), where he introduced dessins d'enfants as combinatorial objects to study the action of the absolute Galois group Gal(Q-bar/Q) on algebraic curves defined over number fields.
%D A398668 A. Grothendieck, Esquisse d'un Programme, 1984.
%D A398668 L. Schneps (ed.), The Grothendieck Theory of Dessins d'Enfants, London Mathematical Society Lecture Note Series 200, Cambridge University Press, 1994.
%e A398668 For n=1, there is a(1) = 1 dessin: the single edge connecting two vertices.
%e A398668 For n=2, there are a(2) = 3 classes (all dessins are defined over Q).
%e A398668 For n=5, there are 70 ordered passports, but a(5) = 74 classes, because some passports contain dessins with different monodromy group orders, indicating different Galois orbits.
%e A398668 For n=6, there are 624 dessins (A057005) and 264 ordered passports, but a(6) = 322 classes, because some passports contain dessins with different monodromy group orders, indicating different Galois orbits.
%o A398668 (Python)
%o A398668 from itertools import permutations
%o A398668 from collections import deque
%o A398668 def A398668(n):
%o A398668     if n == 1: return 1
%o A398668     perms = list(permutations(range(n)))
%o A398668     def ct(p):
%o A398668         vis, res = [0]*n, []
%o A398668         for i in range(n):
%o A398668             if not vis[i]:
%o A398668                 c, j = 0, i
%o A398668                 while not vis[j]: vis[j] = 1; j = p[j]; c += 1
%o A398668                 res.append(c)
%o A398668         return tuple(sorted(res, reverse=True))
%o A398668     def trans(p0, p1):
%o A398668         vis, stk = [0]*n, [0]
%o A398668         vis[0] = 1
%o A398668         while stk:
%o A398668             x = stk.pop()
%o A398668             for p in (p0, p1):
%o A398668                 if not vis[p[x]]: vis[p[x]] = 1; stk.append(p[x])
%o A398668         return sum(vis) == n
%o A398668     def gord(p0, p1):
%o A398668         G = {tuple(range(n))}
%o A398668         q = deque([tuple(range(n))])
%o A398668         while q:
%o A398668             c = q.popleft()
%o A398668             for g in (p0, p1):
%o A398668                 nxt = tuple(c[g[i]] for i in range(n))
%o A398668                 if nxt not in G:
%o A398668                     G.add(nxt)
%o A398668                     q.append(nxt)
%o A398668         return len(G)
%o A398668     classes = set()
%o A398668     for p0 in perms:
%o A398668         for p1 in perms:
%o A398668             if trans(p0, p1):
%o A398668                 comp = [p0[p1[i]] for i in range(n)]
%o A398668                 inv = [0]*n
%o A398668                 for i in range(n): inv[comp[i]] = i
%o A398668                 p_inf = tuple(inv)
%o A398668                 c0, c1, cinf = ct(p0), ct(p1), ct(p_inf)
%o A398668                 classes.add((c0, c1, cinf, gord(p0, p1)))
%o A398668     return len(classes)
%Y A398668 Cf. A057005 (number of dessins d'enfants of degree n).
%K A398668 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398668 1,2
%A A398668 _Jean Lauro Muller_, Sep 15 2026
%E A398668 a(7) from _Sean A. Irvine_, Oct 07 2026
